Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:02.470 align:start position:0%
 
ethiopians are voting in national  elections on monday it's a day of  reckoning for prime minister abby ahmed  as he hopes to boost his legitimacy  with the vote at his first and last  campaign rally this week he said his  main goal was to uphold what he called  the unity and freedom of the country  the election is taking place against a  backdrop of tensions  now let's quickly take a look at how we  got here in 2018 i'll be  road to power on a wave of optimism  after three decades of oppressive role  dominated by the  tigran people's liberation forces or  tplf  a party anchored in the northern region  now after taking office abby made peace  with neighbouring eritrea bringing an  end to a 20-year conflict but that move  earned him  the prestigious nobel peace prize but it  also angered the tplf forces  who neighbor eritrea then last year  abi sent the army into the northern  tegrad region to crush the tplf  thousands have been killed and millions  displaced  to make matters worse ethnic violence  has flared elsewhere hundreds have died  in  clashes on the border between oromia and  amhara the country's two most popular  states  critics accuse abby of denying states  with different ethnicities  more autonomy it's led some opposition  figures  to boycott the poll now we will be  discussing what is at stake for ethiopia  but first we turn to the prime  minister's home region in oromia to find  out what voters there  make of the man  a rally in favor of abi ahmed's  prosperity party  close to his hometown in the aromia  region it was here that crowds of young  people went out on the streets in 2018  to support abi  in this district many still do  based on the work he has achieved we  believe that the prosperity party can  transform this country  the work he did until now shows that he  can bring a lot of things for our future  we believe this party equalizes our  nations and nationalities  despite violence between government  forces and local insurgent groups in  several parts of aromia state  officials in the prime minister's home  village of bashasha  say there's nothing to worry about  we have organized the community leaders  or village leaders  and they are ready to keep the  environment peaceful we are ready to  protect the area from any conflict  abby's birthplace might guarantee safe  pools but in other parts of aromia  unrest is rampant  oromo armed groups actively contest the  holding of elections  claiming they're undemocratic and that  the current government is illegitimate  the two main opposition parties in  oromia decided to withdraw  their main leaders are behind bars this  young man is too scared to speak  publicly  fearing reprisal from authorities he was  among many who celebrated when the prime  minister came to power but he's lost  trust in the government  we are hearing about the loss of so many  lives  and the numbers are increasing day by  day as we come closer to the election  time  we are still hearing that people are  being killed  those who are in prison as well as  opposition parties should be set free  there should be a discussion with the  people and there should be a national  dialogue  i ask the government to do this in a  peaceful way without any loss of life  especially in western aromia clashes  between the oromo liberation army  and federal forces pose a major threat  to the elections  inter-ethnic violence has also left  hundreds dead  and thousands displaced as a consequence  several constituencies will not be able  to vote on june 21st  the international community has warned  of electoral violence  to reduce the risks the european union  held trainings such as this one  to provide tools for peace building  targeting women  youth groups and traditional leaders the  civil society can play  a great role in terms of preventing  managing and mitigating  electoral violence that leadership helps  to play active role in the negotiation  process  they play active role in bringing  different parties  together for building peace that  role also helps them  to have a mediation role through the  vote abby ahmed seeks to establish his  political legitimacy  but the prime minister is facing various  challenges including the conflict in  tigray  for which he's facing mounting pressure  at home  and abroad and for more on ethiopia's  election we've invited ademobe onto the  program he is  an advisor and and an analyst on  democracy in africa he specializes  on ethiopia welcome to dw news africa  doctor  baby we've heard that this election  could chart a new path for ethiopia in  fact prime minister abhi ahmed said  it could build or break the country help  us understand  what is at stake in this election  thank you very much for the invitation  so the the elections are  critical in a sense because the the last  time ethiopia had elections was in 2015  and at that particular time it was a  dominant  uh ruling party that won all the seats  and since then abi ahmed came to power  in april 2018  and he has also established a new  political party  um so this is the first time that abby  ahmed as an individual  and his ruling prosperity party other  party will face the voters  so in that sense it's critical uh for  the ruling party more  more than uh for for the country um in  terms of the  the larger people i think uh i don't  expect the elections to be a mech or  break moment  partly because the two places where the  ruling party could have faced  a tremendous challenge have been  have been affected by different disputes  one in tiguay because of the conflict  and secondly in oromia region which is  the largest region in ethiopia  because of boycotts from the elections  we'll talk more about that in a moment  dr baby  but i first want to ask you how much  does prime minister abi ahmed  need to win this election how much does  he need to be able to say  i have a mandate from the people  um well so he i think that there's  really no question that he's going to  win the elections uh perhaps the key  the question is by how much and he needs  the victory  partly because currently his mandate  originates from  an election in 2015 which which was a  farce  so he wants a renewed mandate  and these elections can provide him that  claim although as i said  there's a lot of issues around the  elections that may affect that claim  dr baby doubts have been raised by the  international community  about the credibility of this election  is this going to be a free and fair poll  um that that certainly was the  expectation when abi yahama came to  power in 2018  and also in 2020 when the elections were  initially supposed to happen  but a lot has happened since then there  is an ongoing conflict in the tiguay  region of  ethiopia and the elections in oromia  have been boycotted by the key  opposition groups  and even in other parts of the country  opposition parties  have faced challenges in terms of  presenting their candidates  and operating freely and fairly so i  think  free and fairness will be a high a high  standard  but if you compare the elections with  the ones that happened  in the past and that they provide quite  a low bar uh we can say that these  elections  would would be would be better perhaps i  think that  the biggest um the biggest beneficiary  if i can say of these elections would be  the electoral board which has developed  tremendous experience  and tremendous expertise in terms of  managing  elections and i think that experience  will be relevant  going forward dr bebe  you you've said that from what you're  saying  an election isn't going to fix the  country what does ethiopia need  uh going on your assumption that prime  minister abby ahmed will win this  election  what's he got to do uh to tackle some of  the  the most pressing issues in the country  and of course that will be the tensions  in the different regions seeking  autonomy  or romeo for one that you have been  referring  um no you you're right i think the  elections uh they  you know you can argue they are  necessary we can argue uh if they are  desirable  but one thing that we know is that the  elections are not going to solve  the the problems of power balance the  political divisions that we see in the  country  they cannot and they are not meant to to  solve those issues  um so if we can uh if the prime minister  abi ahmed  gets a new mandate and a new new claim  of legitimacy  he would do well to engage one in in  rebuilding confidence  because i think that the level of trust  and confidence has suffered  partly as a result of the circumstance  under which the elections are being held  and partly because of other reasons so i  think he would do well to first rebuild  confidence  and then engage in a comprehensive  dialogue to resolve  the key issues between himself  and some opposition parties as well as  those that are outside of the the  electoral competition at the moment  so i think dialogue would be critical  and without it  the elections may not have made much  difference  all right dr adam thank you for your  insight  thank you very much

